<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jiu River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jiu is a river of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, about 340 km long, that rises in the Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a>, cuts through them in a gorge and flows south across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oltenia">Oltenia</a> past Târgu Jiu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craiova">Craiova</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Its upper valley, the Jiu Valley, was Romania's main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-mining area, and in the 13th century the land of the voivode <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/litovoi">Litovoi</a> lay along it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jiu-river.jpg" alt="Jiu River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sandri 90 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jiu_River_in_Craiova.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
